 ],
 "literal": "Even if going near the shore of the wave-dashing ocean / if seeing those who know, thirst-removing / little water people will ask for; like that / even though kings are nearby, faultless / generous patrons poets will seek; therefore / `I too, what is the great gain of that which was obtained?` I will not say / I experienced distress, therefore thinking of you I came / `Give` saying, begging is difficult indeed; you, that / even if you grant, or even if you do not grant, winning wars / from thrown weapons not running away valor, like cloth / pure spread resembling, abundantly high above / many cool cascading down waterfalls, your / having great forest, singing is easy indeed to me",
 "translation": "Even if people walk along the shore of the wave-dashing ocean, if they meet someone who knows where to find it, they will ask for a little fresh water to quench their thirst. In the same way, even when kings are close by, poets seek out faultless, generous patrons. Therefore, I will not say, 'What great gain did I get?' I was in distress, and so, thinking of you, I came. Begging by saying 'Give' is indeed difficult. Whether you grant my request or not, singing of your great forest—with its cool, cascading waterfalls that resemble pure white cloth spread out high above, and your valor that never retreats from thrown weapons in winning wars—is always easy for me.",
 "modern": "அலைகள் மோதும் கடலின் கரைக்கு அருகில் சென்றாலும், மக்கள் தாகம் தீர்க்கும் நல்ல நீரை அறிந்தவரைக் கண்டால் அவரிடம் சிறிதளவு நல்ல நீரையே கேட்பார்கள். அதுபோல, அரசர்கள் அருகில் இருந்தாலும், புலவர்கள் குற்றம் இல்லாத கொடையாளிகளையே தேடிச் செல்வார்கள். அதனால், நானும் 'எனக்குக் கிடைத்த பரிசு என்ன பயன் தரும்?' என்று கேட்க மாட்டேன். வறுமை உற்றேன், அதனால் உன்னை நினைத்து வந்தேன். 'தா' என்று இரப்பது கடினமானது. நீ அதனைத் தந்தாலும் தராவிட்டாலும், போரில் எறியும் படைக்கலன்களுக்கு அஞ்சாத ஆண்மையையும், தூய விரித்த ஆடையைப் போல மலையின் மேல் இருந்து விழும் குளிர்ந்த அருவிகளையும் உடைய உனது பெரிய கானகத்தைப் பாடுவது எனக்கு மிகவும் எளிதானது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the poet Mōcikīraṉār to the chieftain Koṇkāṉaṅ Kiḻāṉ under the theme of 'parisil' (seeking gifts). The poet uses a beautiful simile: just as thirsty travelers walking by the vast but salty ocean will still seek out a small spring of fresh water from those who know, poets will bypass wealthy but ungenerous kings to seek out truly generous patrons. The poet explains that he came to the chieftain because of his own distress, but asserts his dignity by stating that begging is hard. He declares that whether the patron rewards him or not, singing of the chieftain's valor and his beautiful mountain forest—where waterfalls cascade like spread-out white cloth—is always easy and natural for him. Key words include 'வள்ளியோர்' (generous patrons), 'இரத்தலோ அரிதே' (begging is indeed difficult), and 'பாடல் எனக்கு எளிதே' (singing is easy for me).",
